ld leave the office and go and  cry in their car hyperventilate because  frankly the way they were treated drove  them a little bit crazy imagine if every  day you came into work and you had a  colleague who was not as good at their  job they had five years less experience  than you but you know they thought that  your boss is humping other male  colleagues or you know sticking their  balls in their face was funny and that  person got promoted and you didn't that  would drive anyone crazy and it would  make anyone want you know to question  whether they should be at that company  or in the games industry at all there's  a pretty high  emotional and mental costs to working in  a toxic environment being passed up for  promotion for someone less qualified  seems innocuous on the surface but when  it keeps happening I can really [ __ ] you  up  and that's before we get to the most  disgusting behavior one of the things  that a lot of sources told me is that a  lot of these things weren't over I did  hear some instances of sexual predation  or assault at riot but most of what went  on was kind of subtle sort of  microaggressions things like just  talking over women in meetings or a man  presenting the same idea as a woman and  getting praised for it where you know  the woman's idea was kind of shot down  and people didn't talk about this they  didn't feel comfortable addressing  issues of imbalance and how people  treated other peoples different genders  which is really interesting because like  I said before riots culture really  values feedback and direct feedback when  that comes to work and when that comes  to performance but when it comes to kind  of cultural stuff along these lines more  sensitive issues that stuff was kind of  swept under the rug now it wasn't just  specific actions taking against women  either Anastasio reported that one  person riot CEO Scott yelled was a huge  proponent of the generally toxic  environment at Riot Games a lot of  people told me that he was known to  heart on employees faces if women  weren't meetings he would pump male  employees he would ball tap people super  broke culture kind of like fraternity  stuff imagine walking into a meeting and  your boss farts in your face or grabs  your genitals it's legitimately awful I  cannot imagine it the first time you  hear about stuff like that happening you  kind of want to laugh because it sounds  so ridiculous than it be impossible but  if your boss actually did that to you  he'd be living in a nightmare  now following Donna Stassi owes articles  Gelb was put on a two months of leave  but he's still at the company something  a lot of employees allegedly still take  issue with before we get to that though  there's something interesting that I  want to note in August 2018 Donna Stasi  reported that Marc Mero got on stage at  an apology for riots toxic workplace  culture and cried and then Gelb got on  stage and to remind you that  who was allegedly farting on faces and  grabbing genitals it seems kind of wild  to me personally that there's such a  disconnect here that someone can have  such a genuine emotional reaction to a  problem at the place they found it and  then cede the stage to one of the  perpetrators of the issue they feel so  badly about so I asked Anastasio how  does this happen  riot like a lot of tech companies is  very obsessed with the idea or was very  obsessed with the idea of a meritocracy  so the people who are most deserving  should be the most successful and the  most rewarded and nobody would argue  that you know that's not a great idea in  theory like of course people who work  the hardest and are the most talented  and contribute the most to the success  of the company should reap the rewards  of that and receive a lot of praise what  there was no understanding of at the  time supporting the story is why people  of some demographics might have a harder  time fitting into that ideal or  succeeding at the company than others so  for example I grew up as a woman and I  was not somebody who was encouraged to  play video games I was not given video  games by family members I was given  Barbie dolls  I was not encouraged to play  counter-strike and talk on Ventrilo with  my guy friends even though I did that I  didn't have Xbox parties the gaming DNA  is woven into the young male experience  in a way that it's kind of not for women  and that's not because women don't like  video games it's because this is how  games were marketed at that time and I  think Riot was really looking for people  who grew up being gamers and you know PC  master-race  type people and if you are somebody who  might have come to that more recently  and maybe are no less passionate than  somebody who had been gaming their whole  life then things would be more difficult  for you and it might be more difficult  for you to scale the ladder there this  mostly brings us up to last month when  several ride employees walked out of  their office in protest specifically  they were protesting riots policy of  using force binding arbitration in cases  that  involves sexual harassment or  discrimination what that means is that  ride employees didn't have the right to  sue their employer in the case that they  experienced sexual harassment or  discrimination instead they submitted  their case to an arbitrator who made a  final judgment no appeals and Wray gets  to choose the arbitrator that means that  if you experience sexual harassment at  riot say somebody grabbed your genitals  riot picks the person who decides what  happens which puts the power squarely on  a riot side  what employee sued riot for the  harassment and discrimination they faced  at work right try to force them into  arbitration which is what kick-started  the walkouts now before the walkouts  right did actually make a change their  forced arbitration policy new employees  will be able to opt out of forced  arbitration once current litigation is  over that means once riot deals with all  of these lawsuits presumably enforce  arbitration new employees won't have to  deal with all of that that was really  not enough for most riders especially  those involved in that current  litigation so the walkouts went ahead  according to various reports on the  walkouts last month organizers were  demanding an end to forced arbitration  and that many of the people  participating in the walkout felt that  they were tired of ride protecting  people who were responsible for the very  thing they were trying to sue Ryan over  now we reached out to riot and they  mostly linked us to a bunch of material  that's are even out there for a while  but we should still go over it in the  interest of being fair shortly after the  walkout right put out a statement about  what they're doing to answer some of the  issues people had with the company the  first thing is the diversity and  inclusion rider council a group that  right wants to form to keep discussions  about those topics going in a formal  capacity and address the issues that  people have with the company they also  have a group of riders reviewing the  code of conduct to help make it more  representative of the company as a whole  a lot of this is about mitigating and  hopefully preventing the issues that  riders that don estado spoke to faced  and that's admirable there's a lot on  Wright's website about what they're  trying to do to fix those problems but  there's nothing on that website about  ending forced arbitration the most we  got on that specific issue from riot was  this well we ultimately made the choice  not to change our arbitration policy  while in active litigation to give  credit to everyone that participated in  the walkout it's important to point out  that these changes were directly as a  result of what we heard from riders at  walk out and in the lead up to it we  reached out to the walkout organizers  but unfortunately didn't receive a  comment in time for publication but what  we do know from what they said to other  outlets is that there will be further  action speaking to go talk to one of the  organizers Indu Reddy said that they  have plans for further action in that  retaliation is an ongoing concern as of  Monday June 10th Reddy is no longer at  riot she declined to comment as to why  and we're not gonna speculate on that so  what's next well the riot walkout saw a  lot of support from all over the games  industry and another walkout organizer  Jocelyn Monaghan told The Verge that  they have no intention of stopping and  all the support they saw is a big part  of what's keeping them going in my  opinion that's where the fans come in  well it's easy to play a video game and  forget that it's made by humans humans  who love making games and love making  sure that you have the most fun possible  playing them and every cool moment  you've ever had playing League of  Legends has been touched at least in  some small way by one of those people at  riot and they deserve to feel safe at  work anyone should have empathy for the  sources I interviewed for the story and  especially sources who faced harassment  unwanted Commons in any sort of  discrimination I just want people to  think a little bit about how important  it is for the people who make video  games to represent the people who play  them thanks for watching if you want  more content like this hit the sub  button and ring that notification bell  for unique bite-sized videos you won't  find anywhere else hit up our Instagram  Twitter and Facebook pages  [Music]

